WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.404
Logging in: the password is checked once. Then something else must vouch for you.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:14.958
An API asks two questions, always in this order. Who is calling? That's authentication.

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:18.967
And may they do this? That's authorisation.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:30.880
Ana registers with ana@example.com and password 1. The API stores a hash, never the password. 201 Created.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:38.727
Hashing is one-way, and bcrypt adds a random salt, so two equal passwords still look completely different.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:44.435
Registering again with the same email? 409 Conflict.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:53.088
Now she logs in. The API fetches her row, hashes the attempt, and compares it with the stored hash.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:01:03.445
Wrong password: 401, Invalid credentials. An unknown email gets the very same answer, so nobody can discover who has an account.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:07.274
Right password: 200, and a token.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:17.011
But HTTP forgets. The next request knows nothing about her. So something must travel with every request.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:26.250
Option one: a cloakroom. The server keeps your coat and hands you a numbered ticket. That's a session id, in a cookie.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:31.720
The ticket means nothing on its own. The meaning lives in the server's list.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:39.515
Every request, the server looks the ticket up. Log out, and it throws the row away. Instantly.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:50.278
Option two: a signed wristband. Everything is written on the band: who you are, your role, when it expires. 1 hour, here.

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:59.538
The band has three parts, joined by dots: header, payload, signature. The first two are only encoded, not hidden.

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:06.577
The server seals it with its secret signature. Then it keeps nothing. It only checks the seal.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:11.583
Now someone rewrites the band: role: admin.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:19.461
The seal no longer matches. verify() throws invalid signature, and the answer is 401.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:24.094
Anyone can read the band. Only the server can seal it.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:30.631
That is why you never put secrets in a token, and why you always verify, never just decode.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:39.053
The price: a copied band works until it expires. A cloakroom ticket can be cancelled at once.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:45.931
A band needs no lookup, though, and works across many servers. Neither is simply better.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:53.961
With a token, the client sends it on every request, in the Authorization header, after the word Bearer.

00:02:54.310 --> 00:03:02.303
A gate in front of the routes checks it. Valid, and the route runs. Missing or forged, 401.

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:10.782
After the hour is up, the same gate answers 401: jwt expired. Ana logs in again.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:17.351
Practise it with the JWT inspector in Authentication and security.
